  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The layered cup (Peziza varia) is a small to medium-sized cup fungus with a distinctive brownish, irregularly wrinkled appearance. It belongs to the Pezizaceae family and is commonly found across New England's forest floors and disturbed soil areas.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cup Shape with Irregular Edges",
      "description": "Distinctive cup or saucer-like fruiting body with wavy, often lobed margins that distinguish it from other cup fungi",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Wrinkled Inner Surface",
      "description": "The fertile inner surface (hymenium) displays prominent radial wrinkles or ridges extending from the center outward",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Brown Coloration",
      "description": "Exterior and interior surfaces range from light tan to dark brown with a slightly fuzzy outer texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Peziza varia thrives on bare soil, mulch, and disturbed ground in woodlands, gardens, and roadsides throughout New England, particularly in spring and early summer.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite belonging to the same family as desert truffles, the layered cup is a saprotroph that feeds on decaying organic matter rather than forming partnerships with plant roots like its truffle relatives.",
